Description: Self-contained multi-domain scientific computing toolkit for
  observational inference and intervention analysis, hosting the
  Multilevel Reconciliation Methodology ('MRM') framework for Canadian
  carceral, police, and oversight data as its primary application.
  Every statistical algorithm is implemented natively (R and 'C++') and
  cross-validated against its reference implementation; no other
  statistical package is called at runtime.
  Provides general-purpose causal estimators -- average treatment
  effects on the full, treated, and control populations ('ATE', 'ATT',
  'ATC'), group, conditional, and local average treatment effects
  ('GATE', 'CATE', 'LATE'), augmented inverse-probability weighting
  ('AIPW'), and G-computation -- survey sampling methods (stratified,
  cluster, probability-proportional-to-size, bootstrap, calibration
  weights), propensity-score and doubly-robust estimators, and
  sensitivity analyses (E-value, Rosenbaum bounds).
  Companion modules support signal processing and spectral analysis,
  cryptographic primitives, spatial statistics, statistical physics of
  crime (Hawkes self-exciting processes, reaction-diffusion, Levy flight,
  urban scaling), and classical-test-theory and item-response-theory
  psychometrics, alongside ingestion utilities for officially published
  reports of the Ontario Special Investigations Unit ('SIU',
  <https://www.siu.on.ca/>, police oversight) and of federal Structured
  Intervention Units.
  Additional open-data connectors cover the 'CKAN' (Comprehensive
  Knowledge Archive Network, <https://ckan.org/>) portal family
  including Canada's federal portal (<https://open.canada.ca/>),
  Statistics Canada (<https://www.statcan.gc.ca/>), the municipal
  'Socrata' portals of Chicago (<https://data.cityofchicago.org/>),
  New York City (<https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/>), and Calgary
  (<https://data.calgary.ca/>), and the Toronto Police Service
  public-safety data portal (<https://data.tps.ca/>);
  none of these require an account or API (application programming
  interface) key.
